<section xml:id="section-limits-discover" >
<title>Discovering Limits</title>

<p>A major theme of this course is learning properties of curves that make them nice. The first of these properties is a limit existing at a point, or, better, the limit existing on an interval. We say the limit exists on an interval if the limit exists at each point in that interval.</p>

<p>Our first step in understanding when a limit exists is to consider <xref ref="limitsyes" /> which shows examples of points on curves where the limit exists, and <xref ref="limitsno" /> which shows examples of points on curves where the limit does not exist.</p>

<figure xml:id="limitsyes">
	<caption>Each of these limits exist.</caption>

	<sbsgroup>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<p><me>\lim_{t \to 6} f(t)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{y \to \frac{3\pi}{4}} g(y)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{x \to 3.5} h(x)</me></p>
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<image source="images/limitexist1" />
		<image source="images/limitexist2" />
		<image source="images/limitexist3" />
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<p><me>\lim_{x \to 0} f(x)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{x \to 1} f(x)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{t \to 4} h(t)</me></p>
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<image source="images/limitexist4" />
		<image source="images/limitexist5" />
		<image source="images/limitexist6" />
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<p><me> \lim_{\gamma \to 0} f(\gamma)</me></p>
		<p><me> \lim_{\gamma \to 2} f(\gamma)</me></p>
		<p><me> \lim_{\alpha \to 2} h(\alpha)</me></p>
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<image source="images/limitexist7" />
		<image source="images/limitexist7" />
		<image source="images/limitexist8" />
	</sidebyside>
	</sbsgroup>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="limitsno">
	<caption>None of these limits exist.</caption>

	<sbsgroup>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<p><me>\lim_{x \to 2} h(x)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{\alpha \to 1} h(\alpha)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{t \to 0} o(t)</me></p>
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<image source="images/limitexist3" />
		<image source="images/limitexist7" />
		<image source="images/limitexist9" />
	</sidebyside>
	</sbsgroup>
</figure>

<activity xml:id="activity-determine-if-limit">
	<title>Describe Defining Traits of Limits</title>
	<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to determine traits of functions where a limit exists by considering their graphs and to characterize conditions when the limit does not exist.</p></introduction>
	<task><p>For each limit given in <xref ref="limityesno" /> determine if the limit exists or not by finding a matching example in <xref ref="limitsyes" /> for limits that exist or in <xref ref="limitsno" /> for limits that do not exist.</p></task>
	<task><p>Using the examples in <xref ref="limitsyes" />, <xref ref="limitsno" />, and <xref ref="limityesno" /> find traits of the graphs when the limit does exist that are not present in the graphs when the limit does not exist. Likewise find traits of the graphs when the limit does not exist that are not present in the graphs when the limit does exist.</p></task>
</activity>

<figure xml:id="limityesno">
	<caption>Determine which of these limits exist.</caption>

	<sbsgroup>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<p><me>\lim_{x \to 3\pi} f(x)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{\gamma \to 1} h(\gamma)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{\gamma \to 2} h(\gamma)</me></p>
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<image source="images/limitcheck1" />
		<image source="images/limitcheck2" />
		<image source="images/limitcheck2" />
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<p><me>\lim_{t \to 1} h(t)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{t \to 2} h(t)</me></p>
		<p><me>\lim_{t \to \pi/2} \sigma(t)</me></p>
	</sidebyside>
	<sidebyside widths="25%, 25%, 25%">
		<image source="images/limitcheck3" />
		<image source="images/limitcheck3" />
		<image source="images/limitcheck4" />
	</sidebyside>
	</sbsgroup>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-parabolic-salt-pepper">
	<statement>
	<p>Explain why the limit of the following function exists at <m>x=0</m> but not at <m>x=1.</m></p>
	<p><me>p_{sp}(x) = \begin{cases} 0 \amp \text{ if } x \text{ is irrational} \\ x^2 \amp \text{ if } x \text{ is rational} \end{cases}</me></p>
	</statement>
</exercise>

</section>
